# Coding standards

The reviewer applies these to every Rust change. `scripts/check` already enforces formatting,
warnings, the Clippy lints in `Cargo.toml`, doc comments on public items and the dependency
rules, so they are not repeated here.

The reader is {{reader}}. Every rule serves that reader.

## Plain Rust

Use structs, enums, `match`, `Option`, `Result`, iterators and the standard traits, plus the
approved extras below. These need the owner's approval first: `macro_rules!` and procedural
macros, `async`, trait objects and new generic traits, explicit lifetimes beyond what the
compiler infers, `Rc`, `RefCell` and other interior mutability. `unsafe` is forbidden by the
checks. Once approved, an extra goes into the list.

Approved extras:

- None.

Deriving or implementing standard traits is fine, for example `Debug` or `Display`.

## Comments

A reader can follow a module from top to bottom by its comments alone.

- Every module starts with a `//!` comment that says what the module is for.
- Every item has a `///` comment that says what it is for and why.
- Explain a Rust construct briefly the first time a module uses it, if the reader may not know
  it. For example `?`, `impl Trait`, a lifetime or `iter().map()`. One short sentence, sometimes
  with the equivalent in a language the reader knows.
- No filler, no praise of the code, no restating the code in words.

## Errors

- Expected failures are values: return `Result<T, E>` with the module's error enum. The enum
  implements `Display` and `std::error::Error`.
- A panic is for a bug only, never for bad input.
- `main` reports the error and exits non-zero: `fn main() -> ExitCode` or `-> Result<...>`.

## Ownership

- Take `&str` and `&[T]` as arguments, return owned values.
- No `.clone()` just to quiet the borrow checker. Restructure the code instead.

## Functions and names

- Functions are short, do one thing and read top to bottom. Pull out a named helper instead of
  nesting.
- Names say what a value is: `config`, `line_count`. Single letters only in tiny closures.
- Use the same name for the same concept everywhere: in code, tests and docs.

## Dependencies

A new entry or feature in `[dependencies]` or `[dev-dependencies]` in `Cargo.toml` needs the
owner's approval.

## Tests

- New behaviour comes with a test. A bug fix comes with a test that was red before the fix.
- Test names describe behaviour, without a `test_` prefix.
- A rule that holds for all inputs becomes a proptest property.
